Native OpenSSH recovery candidate

This package contains standard OpenSSH configuration and systemd units only.
It has no maintainer scripts, imported keys, account creation, host-key
generation, or automatic activation. Building it does not access a router.

The independent listener is IPv4 port 2222, bound to br0. It permits only
MastermixStudios from 192.168.100.0/24 and only public-key authentication. It
uses the existing native ed25519 host key and the separate persistent file
/var/lib/leon-asus-ssh/recovery_authorized_keys. The ASUS control bridge and
ordinary port 22 neither generate this configuration nor replace this file.

Before activating a reviewed future candidate, separately copy only the
already enrolled public key whose SHA256 fingerprint is
h0AOx4W9yxopLTJeDvC8YdWsiPuhufuDz/DEKY3OH9o into recovery_authorized_keys.
The private key /root/.ssh/netpanel stays on the authorized .51 client.
Make the public file root:root 0644 and its directory ancestors root-owned,
traversable, and not writable by group or others (normally 0755). Preserve
the existing native account, home, PAM policy, shadow, host keys, and identity
marker. Confirm the candidate's actual native host-key fingerprint before
accepting connections; the stock Dropbear fingerprint is a separate identity.

Enable leon-asus-ssh-recovery.socket through its Install WantedBy setting,
which creates sys-subsystem-net-devices-br0.device.wants, not sockets.target.
For an already active br0, explicitly start the socket after enabling it.
For a staged rootfs, create the equivalent device-wants symlink before boot.
br0 can then be created by the broker without the recovery listener holding
up sockets.target or basic.target. Removing br0 stops the listener; the next
device activation starts it again. This requires the bridge to be exposed
as an active systemd device, which must be checked on the target candidate.

Each accepted connection creates OpenSSH's root-owned /run/sshd privilege-
separation directory when needed. Both its template and the package's small
persistent ssh.service.d drop-in set RuntimeDirectoryPreserve=yes, preventing
ordinary SSH shutdown or recovery exit from deleting this directory during
the boot. The ordinary service's listener, ExecStart and generated config
are unchanged; the only shared adjustment is directory preservation.
Missing public authorization or host-key material rejects the connection;
the socket remains available for a later provisioned connection.

Public-key login remains independent of vendor password decryption/import,
but the existing native account and PAM account/session checks must remain
usable. Account expiration, a missing native account, /etc/nologin, missing
br0, firewall rejection, filesystem damage, or systemd failure can still
prevent access. This package changes no sudo policy: sudo can still require
the native account password. It does not grant unrestricted passwordless
root commands or claim privileged recovery after that password is lost.

One new isolated fixture run should check late br0 creation, pinned key-only
login while ordinary SSH/vendor control is unavailable, ordinary ssh.service
stop/start during a live recovery session, br0 removal/recreation, and another
fresh boot. It must also reject password, root, unapproved-key and non-LAN
logins and preserve ordinary port-22 configuration and native account files.
Reuse an existing fixture identity and run no unrelated baseline SSH suite.
Only a later authorized native physical boot can establish target acceptance.

Primary references:
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/systemd/systemd/main/man/systemd.socket.xml
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/systemd/systemd/main/man/systemd.exec.xml
https://man.openbsd.org/sshd
https://man.openbsd.org/sshd_config
